We also notice that the printf call is not secure!

good printf call:
```c
printf("%s", variable);
```

bad printf call:
```c
printf(variable);
```

If a format string is not specified and a variable is unintentionally passed directly to printf, the program may unintentionally expose the contents of its execution stack. In some cases, this even allows reading data from arbitrary addresses within the process’s address space, including the buffer used by our variable.

exemple:

```bash
level3@RainFall:~$ ./level3
-> AAAA %x %x %x %x       
-> AAAA 200 b7fd1ac0 b7ff37d0 41414141
```

The fourth value on the stack, 41414141, corresponds to our injected buffer (AAAA) passed to printf.

We exploit the %n format specifier, which writes the number of characters printed so far to a specified memory address. By supplying the address of the buffer that is later moved into eax before the comparison with 0x40 (64 in decimal), and ensuring that 64 characters are printed, we can influence the outcome of this comparison.

To access the fourth stack element (our supplied address), we use %4$n instead of %n.

Final exploit:
```bash
